{"author":{"id":"29588b87a98184856c6eb9104921c34cca98a9cb029a2ee86cc10622d693ba86"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794875125,"nonce":"ceTbGEvu3OKCaAvHwK5/Zl70C+Y=","title":"Desktop accessibility: expose roles and names to assistive technology (AccessKit or similar)","message":"We are building a shared menu model (menubar, menu, menuitem with checkbox and radio variants, separator, tree/treeitem, toolbar, dialog) on top of Blinc, and we want it to be usable with a screen reader.\n\nWhat we found in the source (checked against the current tree, cf925af): on the desktop platforms Blinc does not pass anything to assistive technology; only the iOS backend does. There are also no roles for menubar, menu, menuitem, menuitemcheckbox, menuitemradio, separator, tree, treeitem, toolbar or dialog. Keyboard operation works, so the menus can be driven, but nothing is announced.\n\nRequest: desktop accessibility support, for example through AccessKit (https://github.com/AccessKit/accesskit), with\n- roles for the list above,\n- accessible names and states (expanded, checked, selected, disabled),\n- keyboard focus events, so a screen reader follows the focused item.\n\nIf it helps, a small prototype or a short guide for wiring a widget to the accessibility tree would already be a good start. We are happy to test with Orca on Linux and NVDA on Windows and to contribute test cases. We are also willing to help with a pull request. There is no hurry on our side.","files":null}]}